the sora machines were the example, not the subject. what this is really
about: three systems, and a directory tree each that fills itself.

someone gets a file. they read it. they delete it. that deletion is them
saying "I read this, next one please?" and the answer is a new file,
chosen completely at random, arriving in the same place the old one was.

they never ask for anything. they never type a command. the only verb
they have is delete, and delete means more.

the deletion travels backwards. it leaves the viewer's inbox, crosses to
the server's outbox, and takes the message with it. a hook notices the
absence and fills it.

three mailboxes, one per system.

the viewer script doesn't need rmail at all. that's the other vision.
this one is rmail with the viewer script applied to it — an
implementation, not an integration. nothing changes on rmail's side.
